  "title": "stored procedures views functions triggers and dynamic SQL",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "Stored procedures package server-side operations and can define parameters, result sets, permissions, and execution context; they do not automatically guarantee safe transactions or good plans.",
    "Views expose a query as a named relational interface. Schema binding and indexed views impose additional constraints; a view is not inherently materialized or ordered.",
    "Functions participate in expressions and queries under different restrictions and optimizer behaviors depending on scalar, inline table-valued, or multi-statement table-valued form.",
    "Triggers execute as side effects of statements and must handle multirow changes correctly. Hidden work and recursion can make triggers difficult to reason about.",
    "Dynamic SQL is code assembled at runtime: bind values as parameters and quote identifiers separately. Escaping data strings manually is not a substitute for parameterization."
  ],
